const UNIT = {
  "gradeLevel": "10",
  "subject": "Core Mathematics",
  "strand": "Strand 2.0: Measurements and Geometry",
  "substrand": "Sub-Strand 2.3: Rotation",
  "totalDuration": "8 lessons × 40 minutes = 320 minutes total",
  "content": "• Properties of rotation (centre, angle, direction — clockwise and anticlockwise)\n• Rotation on a plane surface (using construction to locate images given centre and angle of rotation)\n• Rotation on the Cartesian plane (rotating objects given the centre and angle of rotation using coordinates)\n• Determining the centre and angle of rotation given an object and its image (construction method)\n• Rotational symmetry of plane figures (locating points of symmetry; determining order of rotational symmetry)\n• Axis and order of rotational symmetry in solids (pyramids, triangular prisms, cones, tetrahedrons)\n• Rotation and congruence (deducing congruence — opposite congruence — from rotation)\n• Application of rotation in real-life situations",
  "learningOutcomes": "By the end of the sub-strand, the learner should be able to:\na) determine the properties of rotation in different situations,\nb) rotate an object given the centre and angle of rotation on a plane surface and the Cartesian plane,\nc) determine the centre and angle of rotation given an object and its image,\nd) determine the order of rotational symmetry of plane figures,\ne) determine the axis and order of rotational symmetry in solids,\nf) deduce congruence from rotation,\ng) appreciate the application of rotation in real-life situations.",
  "coreCompetencies": "• Critical Thinking and Problem Solving: the learner draws objects and their images and through construction establishes the centres and angles of rotation, analysing spatial relationships on both plane surfaces and the Cartesian plane.\n• Communication and Collaboration: the learner discusses using an object and its image on a plane surface to generate the properties of rotation, sharing reasoning with peers during group investigations involving clock hands, paper cutouts, and solid figures collected from the environment.",
  "values": "• Responsibility: the learner takes care of learning resources collected from the immediate environment (e.g., pyramids, cones, triangular prisms) to establish their axis and order of rotational symmetry.\n• Respect: learners appreciate each other's opinion as they discuss and draw the image under a rotation given the object, centre and angle of rotation.",
  "sep": "• Developing and Using Models: learners construct geometric models on plane surfaces and the Cartesian plane to represent and predict the position of rotated images, revising their models as they discover properties of rotation.\n• Planning and Carrying Out Investigations: learners carry out structured investigations using improvised clocks, paper cutouts, and collected solids to generate and test the properties of rotational symmetry.\n• Analysing and Interpreting Data: learners analyse coordinate pairs of objects and their images to determine patterns (e.g., invariant centre, preserved distances), deduce the angle of rotation, and identify congruence relationships.",
  "pcis": "• Environmental Education: as the learner responsibly collects regular solids (pyramids, cones, tetrahedrons, triangular prisms) from the immediate environment to determine the axis and order of rotational symmetry, promoting mindful interaction with natural and manufactured objects.",
  "careers": "• Architecture and Engineering: rotation is fundamental in the design of buildings, bridges, and mechanical components — e.g., the rotating turbines at Olkaria Geothermal Plant in the Rift Valley rely on rotational symmetry for efficient energy generation.\n• Graphic Design and Animation: rotation transformations underpin digital design, logo creation, and animation used in Kenya's growing creative industry, including media houses in Nairobi.\n• Manufacturing and Textile Design: rotational patterns are used in fabric and kitenge print design, as well as in the machining of gear-driven equipment in Kenyan agro-processing factories.\n• Aviation and Navigation: pilots and navigators use angular rotation concepts when plotting flight paths and bearing changes, relevant to Kenya's busy airspace managed through Wilson Airport and JKIA in Nairobi.\n• Medicine and Medical Imaging: CT scanners and MRI machines use rotational mechanics; understanding rotation supports careers in biomedical engineering and radiography at hospitals such as Kenyatta National Hospital.",
  "focus": "This unit develops learners' understanding of rotation as a geometric transformation, progressing from hands-on exploration of the properties of rotation using improvised clocks and paper cutouts, to precise construction on plane surfaces and the Cartesian plane, and finally to the identification of rotational symmetry in both plane figures and three-dimensional solids. The Kenyan context is woven throughout — from the rotating hands of the clock tower at the University of Nairobi, to the rotational symmetry of Maasai shields and the gear systems in Nairobi's industrial area — grounding abstract mathematics in familiar, observable phenomena. Learners also deduce congruence from rotation, connecting this sub-strand to the broader theme of geometric transformations in Strand 2.0.",
  "phenomenon": "THE ANCHORING PHENOMENON — \"THE SPINNING HANDS OF NAIROBI'S CLOCK TOWER\"\n\nStudents are shown a short video clip (or a large printed image sequence) of the clock on the Administration Block of the University of Nairobi. The clip captures the minute hand at four distinct positions over one hour: 12:00, 12:15, 12:30, and 12:45. The hand is clearly the same length and shape at every position, yet it points in a completely different direction each time.\n\nStudents are asked to observe and discuss: The minute hand has moved — but what exactly has changed, and what has stayed the same? The tip of the hand traces a perfect circular arc. The hand itself does not stretch, shrink, or flip. Every point on the hand remains exactly the same distance from the centre of the clock face. Yet the hand has unmistakably moved.\n\nThis is puzzling because it challenges learners to articulate what kind of motion this is — it is neither a slide (translation) nor a mirror flip (reflection). Something is turning about a fixed point. The questions that naturally arise are: Where is the fixed point? How much has the hand turned? Could we predict exactly where the hand will be after any given amount of time? And — if we placed the hand on a coordinate grid, could we calculate the new position of every point on it mathematically?\n\nThis phenomenon anchors the entire unit: every lesson returns to the idea of a fixed centre, a measurable angle, a preserved shape and size, and a predictable new position — the four pillars of rotation as a geometric transformation.",
  "supportingPhenomena": "• The rotating blades of a maize grinding mill (posho mill) in a Kisumu market: learners observe that each blade tip traces a circle around a fixed axle — prompting questions about the centre of rotation, the angle swept per second, and the symmetry of the blade arrangement.\n• Rotational patterns on a traditional Kikuyu/Maasai woven basket (kiondo): learners examine how the same motif repeats at equal angular intervals around a central point, introducing the concept of order of rotational symmetry in a culturally familiar object.\n• A football (soccer ball) rolling across the pitch at Kasarani Stadium in Nairobi: learners notice that as the ball rolls, a mark on its surface traces a curved path — the mark rotates about the ball's moving centre, connecting rotation to real-world circular motion.\n• The rotating spray arm inside a water-pumping windmill on a Rift Valley farm: learners observe that the arm returns to its original orientation after a full 360° turn, and that opposite arms are congruent images of each other — linking rotation to both symmetry and congruence.",
  "drivingQuestion": "DRIVING QUESTION: When the minute hand of the clock at the University of Nairobi completes its journey around the clock face, what mathematical rules govern exactly where every point on the hand ends up — and how can we use those rules to rotate any shape to any position, on paper and on a grid?\n\nKICD KEY INQUIRY QUESTION:\n1. How is rotation applied in real-life situations?",
  "storylineThread": "Lesson 1 — PREDICT: \"What is happening to the clock hand?\" | Learners observe the anchoring phenomenon (clock tower video/image sequence). In groups they predict what has changed and what has stayed the same. They sketch the hand at each position and attempt to describe the motion in their own words. The teacher records predictions on the Driving Question Board (DQB). Key vocabulary — centre, angle, direction, clockwise, anticlockwise — is introduced through discussion. Learners use an improvised clock (a card arrow pinned to a board) to demonstrate and discover that distance from centre is preserved.\n\nLesson 2 — OBSERVE & EXPLORE: \"Discovering the Properties of Rotation\" | Learners use paper cutouts of plane figures (triangles, quadrilaterals) and a pin (as the centre of rotation) to physically rotate objects through specified angles on plain paper. They record observations: object and image are congruent; every point moves through the same angle; the centre is the only invariant point; direction matters. Groups share findings and a class list of Properties of Rotation is compiled and added to the DQB. Connection to the posho mill supporting phenomenon is made.\n\nLesson 3 — EXPLAIN & PRACTISE: \"Rotating Objects on a Plane Surface\" | Learners use a geometrical set (ruler, compass, protractor) to construct the image of a given object under a specified rotation (centre and angle given) on plain paper. Step-by-step construction method is developed collaboratively: locate centre, draw rays from centre through each vertex, measure the angle, mark the image vertex at equal distance. Multiple examples with different centres (inside, outside, on the object) and angles (90°, 180°, 270°) are practised. Learners connect this to the clock-hand phenomenon — the centre of the clock is always fixed.\n\nLesson 4 — EXPLAIN & PRACTISE: \"Rotation on the Cartesian Plane\" | Learners transfer the construction skill to the Cartesian plane. Using coordinate grids, they rotate objects about the origin and about other specified centres through 90°, 180°, and 270° (clockwise and anticlockwise). They discover and record coordinate rules (e.g., 90° anticlockwise about the origin: (x, y) → (−y, x)) through guided pattern-spotting. The Kasarani football rolling phenomenon is revisited — learners calculate where a marked point would appear after a 90° and 180° rotation.\n\nLesson 5 — INVESTIGATE: \"Finding the Centre and Angle of Rotation\" | Given only an object and its image (no centre or angle provided), learners use perpendicular bisector construction to locate the centre of rotation and a protractor to measure the angle. Group investigation on plain paper and Cartesian plane. Results are verified by rotating the object using the found centre and angle and checking it maps onto the image. Learners return to the DQB and update: \"We can work backwards — find the centre and angle from the evidence.\"\n\nLesson 6 — EXPLORE: \"Rotational Symmetry of Plane Figures and Solids\" | Learners use paper cutouts of regular polygons (equilateral triangle, square, regular hexagon — connecting to patterns on a Maasai kiondo basket) to locate points of rotational symmetry and determine the order of rotational symmetry for each figure. They then collect or examine models of 3-D solids (pyramids, triangular prisms, cones, tetrahedrons — connecting to the Rift Valley windmill phenomenon) and identify the axis and order of rotational symmetry for each solid. Findings are recorded in a class table on the DQB.\n\nLesson 7 — EXPLAIN: \"Rotation and Congruence\" | Learners use pairs of object-and-image cutouts from previous lessons to classify the type of congruence produced by rotation (opposite congruence — the image cannot be superimposed without lifting from the plane, unlike direct congruence from translation). They compare this with congruence from reflection (also opposite) and from translation (direct). A sorting activity consolidates understanding. Learners connect to the clock: the hour hand at 3:00 and at 9:00 are congruent — but one is a rotation of the other, not a reflection.\n\nLesson 8 — MODEL BUILDING, APPLICATION & ASSESSMENT: \"Putting It All Together\" | Learners revisit the anchoring phenomenon: given the clock hand position at 12:00 on a coordinate grid, they calculate its exact position at 12:15, 12:30, 12:45, and 1:00 using rotation rules. They then complete a structured class activity covering all seven SLOs: rotate an object on the Cartesian plane, find a centre and angle of rotation, determine rotational symmetry order of a given figure and solid, and identify congruence from rotation. Final DQB review: learners articulate a full answer to the driving question in writing. Real-life appreciation task: identify two examples of rotation in their school or home environment in Nairobi/their locality and describe the centre, angle, and order of symmetry."
};

