    "teacherReflection": "After the lesson, reflect on the following questions: (1) When learners compared their Lesson 1 sketches with their final models, were they able to articulate specific conceptual additions (e.g., coordinate rules, construction methods, symmetry order) rather than just saying 'I know more now'? If not, how might future lessons better scaffold metacognitive language throughout the unit rather than only at the end? (2) During Part A of the class activity, how many learners applied the correct clockwise rule (x,y)→(y,−x) without prompting? If fewer than 70% of the class did so correctly, consider incorporating a quick daily rotation-rule drill in the opening 3 minutes of future geometry lessons. (3) Did learners' written answers to the driving question (Part G and the DQB sticky note) demonstrate genuine synthesis — connecting all four pillars of rotation (fixed centre, measurable angle, preserved shape/size, predictable new position) — or did they describe only one or two ideas? Use this to inform the framing of the driving question for future cohorts. (4) Were the real-life examples learners identified in the take-home task drawn from their genuine local Nairobi/Kenyan context (ceiling fans, mkokoteni wheels, jiko components, school gate hinges) or did they default to Western textbook examples? Kenyan contextual fluency in mathematical thinking is a goal of CBE and should be celebrated and reinforced when present. (5) Consider whether a brief peer-assessment of the Model Comparison Cards using a simple 3-point rubric (Developing / Meeting / Exceeding) in the following lesson would deepen learners' understanding of what a strong final explanation looks like in mathematics.",
