      "explained": "The minute hand of the University of Nairobi clock is a real-world example of rotation: a geometric transformation in which every point of an object turns through a fixed angle about a fixed centre (the origin/clock centre), preserving all distances and angles. The mathematical rules that govern exactly where every point ends up are coordinate rotation rules: for a 90° clockwise rotation, (x,y)→(y,−x); for 180°, (x,y)→(−x,−y); for 270° clockwise, (x,y)→(−y,x). These rules allow us to predict the precise location of every point on any shape after any rotation on the Cartesian plane. The object and its image are always directly congruent (same shape, same size, same orientation type). The clock hand itself has rotational symmetry of order 1 (as a non-symmetric shape), while the circular clock face has infinite rotational symmetry — both are explained by the same underlying mathematical structure of rotation."
