      "exemplar": "To rotate a full shape, you apply the rotation rule to every vertex (corner point) of the shape, one at a time. Here is the complete method, using triangle KNB — named after Kisumu, Nairobi, and Baringo — with vertices K(1, 2), N(4, 2), and B(4, 5). We will rotate it 90° anticlockwise about the origin.\n\nSTEP 1 — DRAW AND LABEL THE OBJECT.\nPlot points K(1, 2), N(4, 2), and B(4, 5) on your grid. Join them to form triangle KNB. This is your object.\n\nSTEP 2 — APPLY THE ROTATION RULE TO EACH VERTEX.\nUsing the rule for 90° anticlockwise: (x, y) → (−y, x)\n• K(1, 2) → K'(−2, 1)\n• N(4, 2) → N'(−2, 4)\n• B(4, 5) → B'(−5, 4)\n\nSTEP 3 — PLOT AND LABEL THE IMAGE.\nPlot K'(−2, 1), N'(−2, 4), and B'(−5, 4) on the same grid. Join them to form triangle K'N'B'. Label the image clearly using prime notation (the dash symbol ').\n\nSTEP 4 — CHECK YOUR WORK.\n• Measure the side lengths of the object and the image — they must be equal (isometry).\n• Check that every image point is the same distance from the origin as the corresponding object point: distance of K from origin = √(1² + 2²) = √5; distance of K' from origin = √((−2)² + 1²) = √5. ✓\n• The image should look like the object has been turned, not stretched or flipped.\n\nSTEP 5 — STATE THE TRANSFORMATION FULLY.\nAlways describe the transformation with all four pillars: 'Triangle KNB has been rotated 90° anticlockwise about the origin to give triangle K'N'B'.'\n\nJust as the minute hand of the Nairobi clock remains the same length no matter where it points, your triangle remains the same shape and size no matter how far it has been rotated."
