      "prompt": "Now answer the driving question directly and completely. Your answer should bring together all four pillars of rotation, the coordinate rules, the method for rotating a shape, and the real-world relevance. Write at least two well-developed paragraphs. Imagine you are explaining to a fellow student who has never studied rotation before.",
      "exemplar": "When the minute hand of the clock at the University of Nairobi completes its journey around the clock face, four mathematical rules govern exactly where every point on the hand ends up. First, every point rotates around one fixed centre — the pivot at the middle of the clock face, which on a coordinate grid we model as the origin (0, 0). Second, every point on the hand turns through the same angle — 90° for every 15 minutes, and a full 360° for one complete hour. Third, the direction matters: the hand moves clockwise, meaning from the top (12) toward the right (3), downward (6), and back to the top. Fourth, and most importantly, the hand is perfectly preserved — it never changes length, shape, or size, no matter where it points. These four pillars — centre, angle, direction, and preservation — are the complete mathematical description of rotation as a geometric transformation.\n\nTo use these rules on a coordinate grid, you apply the rotation formulae to every point of the shape. For a 90° clockwise rotation about the origin, the rule is (x, y) → (y, −x). For 90° anticlockwise, it is (x, y) → (−y, x). For 180°, it is (x, y) → (−x, −y). You apply whichever rule matches your angle and direction to every vertex of the shape, plot the new points, join them, and label them with prime notation. The result is an image that is exactly congruent to the original — same side lengths, same angles — just turned to a new position. If the centre of rotation is not the origin, you translate the shape first, apply the rule, then translate back. To work backwards — finding the centre and angle when only the object and image are given — you construct perpendicular bisectors of the segments joining each vertex to its image, and measure the angle at their intersection. Every time you carry out any of these steps, you are doing exactly what the Nairobi clock does every second: turning a shape through a precise angle about a fixed point, landing it in a perfectly predictable new position, without changing a single measurement within it."
